window.docContents[177].resolve({"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___1___-What-is-representation-theory___/Overview-of-representation-theory___-associative-algebras___-representations___-subrepresentations___-direct-sums/#Chapter2___Discussion_2___1_overview":{"contents":"In technical terms, representation theory studies representations of associative algebras. Its\ngeneral content can be very briefly summarized as follows.\n\nAn associative algebra over a field k is a vector space A over k equipped with an\nassociative bilinear multiplication a, b \\mapsto ab, a, b \\in A. We will always consider\nassociative algebras with unit, i.e., with an element 1 such that 1 \\cdot a = a \\cdot 1 = a\nfor all a \\in A. A basic example of an associative algebra is the algebra \\operatorname{End} V\nof linear operators from a vector space V to itself. Other important examples include\nalgebras defined by generators and relations, such as group algebras and universal enveloping\nalgebras of Lie algebras.\n\nA representation of an associative algebra A (also called a left A-module) is a\nvector space V equipped with a homomorphism \\rho : A \\to \\operatorname{End} V, i.e., a linear\nmap preserving the multiplication and unit.\n\nA subrepresentation of a representation V is a subspace U \\subset V which is invariant\nunder all operators \\rho(a), a \\in A. Also, if V_1, V_2 are two representations of A,\nthen the direct sum V_1 \\oplus V_2 has an obvious structure of a representation of A.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t2. Basic notions of representation theory\t2.1. What is representation theory?\tOverview of representation theory: associative algebras, representations, subrepresentations, direct sums","header":"Overview of representation theory: associative algebras, representations, subrepresentations, direct sums","id":"/2___-Basic-notions-of-representation-theory/2___1___-What-is-representation-theory___/Overview-of-representation-theory___-associative-algebras___-representations___-subrepresentations___-direct-sums/#Chapter2___Discussion_2___1_overview"},"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___3___-Representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras/Irreducible-representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras/#Chapter3___Theorem3___3___1":{"contents":"Theorem 3.3.1. Let A = \\bigoplus_{i=1}^r \\operatorname{Mat}_{d_i}(k). Then the irreducible representations of A are V_1 = k^{d_1}, \\ldots, V_r = k^{d_r}, and any finite dimensional representation of A is a direct sum of copies of V_1, \\ldots, V_r.\n\n\n\n","context":"Introduction to Representation Theory\t3. General results of representation theory\t3.3. Representations of direct sums of matrix algebras\tIrreducible representations of direct sums of matrix algebras","header":"Irreducible representations of direct sums of matrix algebras","id":"/3___-General-results-of-representation-theory/3___3___-Representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras/Irreducible-representations-of-direct-sums-of-matrix-algebras/#Chapter3___Theorem3___3___1"}});
